1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Triage a cross-database incident. Where did progress stop, and which way does the repair go?

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:09,200
A resident complains. First, recall the design, not the symptom.

3
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:15,800
The owner is known before the incident: PostgreSQL. So trace from there.

4
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:18,366
published proves the relay sent it, not that anyone received it

5
00:00:18,866 --> 00:00:20,933
received: the event reached the consumer

6
00:00:21,433 --> 00:00:24,366
a timeout alone is ambiguous: the write may or may not have happened

7
00:00:24,866 --> 00:00:26,933
the retry path cannot make progress

8
00:00:27,433 --> 00:00:30,433
this later read establishes that the projection is behind

9
00:00:30,933 --> 00:00:36,333
Committed, recorded, published, received... but not applied.

10
00:00:36,833 --> 00:00:43,233
Agreement from the wrong direction is not a repair.

11
00:00:43,733 --> 00:00:45,733
Because the consumer is idempotent and version-guarded, replay is safe.

12
00:00:46,233 --> 00:00:53,233
"Or newer": another accepted update may have arrived while it was paused.

13
00:00:53,733 --> 00:00:55,233
A count check passes: 3 and 3.

14
00:00:55,733 --> 00:00:58,600
Compare the UNION of IDs, then the owned fields: status and version.

15
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:01,600
1010 has equal versions and a wrong status: comparing versions alone misses it.

16
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:09,500
Investigate 9999 before deleting it. Rebuild from the owner, then verify.

17
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,500
Label the repair as proposed until you have checked it.

18
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:24,100
Remember: Start from the owner: PostgreSQL holds the accepted status. Follow one event ID: committed, recorded, published, received, applied. A timeout is ambiguous. A later read is evidence. Repair the stuck path; never revert the owner to match a stale copy. Equal counts prove little: compare IDs and owned values.

